/*
RMSTEST_31 - do cliff tiles count as foreign terrain for terrain spacing
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 12, Sec.6.4, Sec.9 item 7

QUESTION
Sec.9 item 7 describes an under-cliff terrain-16 mechanic. Three exports with
33-56 cliff units each did NOT show it: every terrain histogram summed to
exactly dim squared with no terrain 16 anywhere. Not simulating a mechanic that
does not appear costs nothing.

The problem is what was resting on it. Sec.6.4 approximates cliff tiles as
counting as foreign terrain for spacing_to_other_terrain_types, and that
approximation was justified BY the terrain-16 mechanic - if a cliff overwrites
the tile beneath it, of course a spacing rule sees foreign terrain there. The
justification is gone. The behaviour may well survive on its own, but right now
Sec.6.4 carries a rule with no reason behind it, which is the state most likely
to be quietly wrong.

METHOD
One terrain generated with a large spacing_to_other_terrain_types, on a map that
also generates cliffs. If cliffs count as foreign terrain, SNOW keeps its
distance from them. If they do not, SNOW ignores them entirely and patches sit
right up against cliff units.

<CLIFF_GENERATION> IS PRESENT AND POPULATED HERE, deliberately, and this is the
one script in the batch where that is correct. Note the trap it comes from: an
EMPTY cliff header still generates cliffs, which is what contaminated the
2026-08-01 batch. Here we want cliffs and we say so.

Cliffs are OBJECTS in the export, not terrain - that is what the three earlier
exports established. So this needs two reads and a comparison between them,
rather than a single terrain histogram.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count.

FIRST, FIND THE CLIFF OBJECT'S NAME
Run the probe with no flags and read the default object histogram. The cliff
entry's name is whatever that prints - do not guess it, and do not assume it
matches a constant in game-constants.json, which holds 31 of several hundred.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. If cliffs DO count as foreign terrain: the minimum distance from any SNOW
   tile to any cliff unit is at or above the declared spacing of 6. Sec.6.4's
   rule stands and gets a new justification that does not depend on terrain 16.
2. If they do NOT: SNOW patches abut cliff units, with minimum distances of 0
   or 1. Sec.6.4's approximation is wrong and should be deleted rather than
   re-justified.
3. Confirm again that no terrain 16 appears and that the terrain histogram sums
   to exactly 40000. Two independent exports already showed this; a third at a
   different map size makes it a property rather than a coincidence.

THE ORTHOGONAL READING, which is the one most likely to be interesting
Also check whether cliffs avoid SNOW - i.e. whether the constraint runs the
other way. Cliff generation and terrain generation are different stages (S3 and
S4), and Sec.9 item 7 puts cliffs BEFORE terrain, so a spacing rule declared on
the terrain cannot have influenced where the cliffs went. If the cliffs turn out
to be the ones doing the avoiding, the stage order in Sec.9 is wrong, which
would matter far more than the spacing rule this run was written to settle.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>"                       (find cliff name)
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --clusters <cliffname>
  Then take the minimum distance between any SNOW patch tile and any cliff
  position. The patch bounding boxes are enough for a first pass; if they
  overlap, the answer is already (2).

RUN IT THREE TIMES.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<CLIFF_GENERATION>
  min_number_of_cliffs 20
  max_number_of_cliffs 30
  min_length_of_cliff 4
  max_length_of_cliff 9

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  create_terrain SNOW
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    land_percent 8
    number_of_clumps 12
    spacing_to_other_terrain_types 6
    set_scale_by_groups
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
